course: money
lesson: 13
title: Unit prices and sales that are not really sales
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: To compare a small pack and a big pack fairly, work out the price for the same amount, called the unit price: four small 250 ml juices at 50p make a litre for £2, but the 1 litre bottle is £1.60, so the big one saves 40p a litre.
explain: Big is not always cheaper: a 100 g pack for £1 is 1p a gram, while a 300 g "giant" pack for £3.30 is 1.1p a gram, so the giant one is dearer per gram; shelf labels often show the unit price in small print.
explain: Big "SALE!!!" signs, "was £600, now £400" and "up to 70% off" can mislead: the trainers may be £60 before and after, and "up to" may mean just two things at 70% off and the rest at 10%.
explain: Watch for shrinkflation, when a bar shrinks from 200 g to 180 g at the same £2, and remember that a picture on a box is an advert, while the ingredients list tells the truth.
fact: The unit price is the price for one gram, one litre, or one item; it lets you compare packs of different sizes fairly.
fact: Bigger packs are often cheaper per unit, but not always.
fact: A sign that says "sale" does not prove the price has fallen; compare the real prices.
fact: Some shops raise a price before a sale so that the discount looks bigger; price-tracking websites show what something really cost before.
fact: Black Friday is a big shopping sale day in late November.
fact: Shrinkflation is when a product gets smaller but the price stays the same.
fact: Multi-packs are not always cheaper: divide the pack price by the number of items to compare.
fact: "Up to 70% off" means the biggest discount is only on some things; most things are cut by less.
fact: Pictures on packets are adverts; the ingredients list shows what is really inside.
fact: Corner shops often charge more than big supermarkets because they buy smaller amounts and stay open late near you; you pay for convenience.
fact: "Limited edition" often means the same product in new packaging, sold to make you buy it now.
fact: "Dearer" is a British word for more expensive.
exercise: Small juice: 250 ml for 50p. How much do 4 small ones (1 litre) cost? | £2.00 | £1.60 | £1.50 | £2.50
exercise: The 1 litre bottle costs £1.60. How much does it save compared with 4 small ones? | 40p | 60p | 20p | 50p
exercise: What is a unit price? | The price for one gram, litre or item | The price of the biggest pack on the shelf | The price at the till after a sale is taken off | The price of one shop's own-brand version
exercise: A 100 g pack costs £1. What is the price per gram? | 1p | 10p | 0.1p | £1
exercise: A 300 g giant pack costs £3.30. What is the price per gram? | 1.1p | 1p | 3.3p | 11p
exercise: Trainers were £60 last week and are £60 under the "SALE!!!" sign. How much is off? | £0 | £60 | £6 | £30
exercise: A TV says "£400, was £600", but it cost £400 most of the year. What is the real saving? | £0 | £200 | £600 | £400
exercise: A chocolate bar shrank from 200 g to 180 g but still costs £2. How much chocolate is lost? | 20 g | 180 g | 2 g | 10 g
exercise: What is shrinkflation? | A product gets smaller while the price stays the same | Prices getting smaller every year | A shop shrinking its opening hours | Clothes shrinking in the wash
exercise: A pack of 6 pens costs £3. How much is each pen? | 50p | 30p | 60p | 40p
exercise: Single pens are 40p. Which is cheaper per pen? | The single pens | The pack of 6 | They are exactly the same | You cannot tell
exercise: A £20 jumper is 10% off in an "up to 70% off" sale. What does it cost? | £18 | £6 | £14 | £10
exercise: What does "up to 70% off" really mean? | Only some things are 70% off; most are less | Everything in the whole shop is 70% off today | Most things are even more than 70% off the price | You get 70% of your money back when you pay
exercise: A can is £1 at the corner shop and 60p at the supermarket. How much dearer is the corner shop? | 40p | 60p | 30p | £1.60
exercise: Why are corner shops often dearer? | They buy smaller amounts and are open late near you | They want to cheat their customers | Their cans are bigger | The law makes them charge more
exercise: A limited-edition sweet costs £2 and the normal one £1. How much extra is the gold wrapper? | £1 | £2 | £3 | 50p
exercise: Where can you find out what is really inside a cereal box? | The ingredients list | The big picture on the front | The advert you saw on TV | The colour of the box
test: "Look, Mum! Giant pack, so cheap!" Mum points at the tiny writing. What's she checking? | The unit price, which shows it's dearer per gram | The use-by date, which says it's off | The barcode, which shows it's fake | The weight, which shows it's empty
test: Dean goes "mate, that's not a sale" about £60 trainers under a SALE sign. Is he right? | Yes, the price didn't change at all | No, every sale sign means a lower price | No, the sign makes them cheaper at the till | Yes, they actually went up to £70 before the sale
test: Nan: "they've nicked a chunk!" Her bar went from 200 g to 180 g, still £2. If she buys 10 bars, how much chocolate does she lose in all? | 200 g | 20 g | 180 g | 2 kg
test: Your mate: "Big packs are ALWAYS cheaper, everyone knows that." What's the truth? | Often, but not always; check the unit price | Yes, always, because it's a law that big packs are cheaper | No, big packs are always dearer | Only on Black Friday
test: Rob's dad checks a price-tracking site on Black Friday. Why? | To see if the "was £600" price was ever real | To find a shop nearby that's still open late tonight | To order a second telly for the bedroom at half price | To read reviews of the new film at the cinema
test: A Geordie mate says "that's a canny deal, man, six pens for three quid!" ("Canny" means good.) Singles are 40p. Is it canny? | No, the pack is 50p a pen, singles are 40p | Yes, it's 30p a pen | Yes, because packs of pens are always cheaper than singles | No, the pack is £1 a pen
test: Sam yells "lies!" at a cereal box covered in strawberries with only 3 g of fruit in every 100 g. What's going on? | The picture is an advert; the ingredients tell the truth | The shop stole the strawberries | The strawberries melted in the box | Pictures on boxes must show exactly what's inside
test: Aidan needs 6 cans: £1 each at the corner shop, 60p at the big supermarket. How much does he save at the supermarket? | £2.40 | £3.60 | 40p | £6.00
test: What would happen if nobody ever checked "was" prices? | Shops could raise prices before sales and fake big discounts | Prices would always fall by half | Sales would stop happening | Every price would become fair
test: Gran sniffs at a £2 limited-edition sweet, "it's the same flipping sweet." A Gen Z cousin says "it's giving scam vibes, lowkey." ("It's giving" means it seems like.) What's the honest view? | Often it's the same sweet in new packaging, so it's your choice | Limited editions are always made of better stuff | The normal sweet must be fake | Limited editions are illegal
test: Juice: 500 ml for 90p or 1 litre for £1.60. Which is cheaper per litre? | The 1 litre bottle (£1.60 against £1.80) | The 500 ml bottle (90p against £1.60) | They are exactly the same | The 500 ml bottle (£1.80 a litre against £1.60)
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "unit price"? | The price expressed in respect of a single gram, litre or item | The price of the largest package available | The price displayed upon a sign bearing the word "sale" | The price prevailing upon Black Friday only
exam: It is given that a product of two hundred grams is reduced to one hundred and eighty grams whilst its price of two pounds is maintained. Identify the phenomenon. | Shrinkflation | A genuine reduction in price | A loyalty reward | A bundle offer
exam: It is given that four containers of two hundred and fifty millilitres are priced at fifty pence each, and a single container of one litre at one pound sixty pence. Ascertain the saving per litre afforded by the larger container. | Forty pence | Sixty pence | Twenty pence | Fifty pence
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the inscription "up to seventy per cent off". | The maximal reduction applies to some articles only, most being reduced less | Every article is reduced by precisely seventy per cent | Most articles are reduced by more than seventy per cent | Seventy per cent of the purchase price is refunded
